('POL-NF-00211', 'Cannabinoids', null, null, '[{"part":"Whole","status":"NOT_YET_AUTHORISED_NOVEL_FOOD"}]'::jsonb, 'The hemp plant ( Cannabis sativa L.) contains a number of cannabinoids and the most common ones are as follows: delta-9-tetrahydrocannabinol (Δ9-THC), its precursor in hemp, delta-9-tetrahydrocannabinolic acid A (Δ9-THCA-A), delta-9-tetrahydrocannabinolic acid B (Δ9-THCA-B), delta-8-tetrahydrocannabinol (Δ8-THC), cannabidiol (CBD), its precursor in hemp cannabidiolic acid (CBDA), cannabigerol (CBG), cannabinol (CBN), cannabichromene (CBC), and delta-9-tetrahydrocannabivarin (Δ9-THCV). Without pr'),
('POL-NF-00212', 'Cannabis sativa L.', null, 'Hemp (EN)', '[{"part":"Seeds","status":"NOT_NOVEL_IN_FOOD"},{"part":"Water infusion of hemp leaves (when not accompanied by the flowering and fruiting tops) consumed as such or as part of herbal infusions","status":"NOT_NOVEL_IN_FOOD"},{"part":"Soluble edestin from Cannabis sativa L.","status":"NOT_YET_AUTHORISED_NOVEL_FOOD"}]'::jsonb, 'Hemp seeds, hemp seed oil, ground hemp seeds, (partially) defatted hemp seeds and other hemp seed-derived food for which a history of consumption has been demonstrated are not novel. Maximum levels of delta-9-tetrahydrocannabinol equivalents and additional requirements on food information in hemp seed and seed-derived food products, hemp leaves for water infusion and ready to drink hemp leaves infusions are set by Commission Regulation (EU) 2023/915 on maximum levels for certain contaminants in '),
('POL-NF-00112', 'Althaea officinalis', 'Althaea kragujevacensis Pančić', 'Marshmallow (EN)', '[{"part":"Whole","status":"NOT_NOVEL_IN_FOOD_SUPPLEMENTS"}]'::jsonb, 'The entry concerns the whole plant ‘Althaea officinalis L.’ It belongs to the Malvaceae Family.'),
('POL-NF-00113', 'Amaranthus caudatus', 'Amaranthus abyssinicus hort. ex L.H.Bailey , Amaranthus paniculatus', 'prince’s feather (EN)', '[{"part":"Seeds, fruits and leaves","status":"NOT_NOVEL_IN_FOOD"}]'::jsonb, 'The entry concerns the seeds, fruits and leaves of Amaranthus caudatus L. It belongs to the Amaranthaceae Family.'),
('POL-NF-00114', 'Amaranthus cruentus', 'Amaranthus anacardana Hook.f.', 'graanamarant (NL)', '[{"part":"Fruits and seeds","status":"NOT_NOVEL_IN_FOOD"}]'::jsonb, 'The entry concerns the fruits and seeds of Amaranthus cruentus L. It belongs to the Amaranthaceae Family.'),
('POL-NF-00115', 'Amaranthus edulis', null, null, '[{"part":null,"status":null}]'::jsonb, 'see Amaranthus hypocondriacus'),
('POL-NF-00214', 'Capparis spinosa', null, 'Caper, caperbush, caper plant, caperberry (EN)', '[{"part":"Fruit, leaves, flower buds","status":"NOT_NOVEL_IN_FOOD"}]'::jsonb, 'The flower buds, fruits (berries) and leaves of Capparis spinosa (caper) have been used as food within the EU before 15 May 1997. Capparis spinosa , the caper bush, is a perennial winter-deciduous species that bears rounded, fleshy leaves and large white to pinkish-white flowers. It belongs to the plant family Capparidaceae (Capparaceae). A caper is also the pickled bud of this plant. Caper bush is present in almost all the circum-Mediterranean countries and is included in the floristic composit'),
('POL-NF-00215', 'Capsicum frutescens', null, 'Tabasco pepper, chili pepper, bird pepper, cayenne pepper (EN)', '[{"part":"Fruits","status":"NOT_NOVEL_IN_FOOD"},{"part":"Sapwood and resin","status":"NOT_NOVEL_IN_FOOD_SUPPLEMENTS"}]'::jsonb, 'The entry concerens the fruits (berries), sapwood and resin of Capsicum frutescens.'),
('POL-NF-00216', 'Caralluma fimbriata', null, null, '[{"part":"Whole","status":"NOT_NOVEL_IN_FOOD_SUPPLEMENTS"}]'::jsonb, 'Caralluma fimbriata is considered as not novel in food supplements .'),
('POL-NF-00217', 'Carduus benedictus', null, null, '[{"part":null,"status":null}]'::jsonb, 'see Cnicus benedictus'),
('POL-NF-00218', 'Carica papaya', null, 'Papaya, papayero, mamón (ES)', '[{"part":"Fruit oil","status":"SUBJECT_TO_A_CONSULTATION_REQUEST"},{"part":"Seeds and leaves","status":"NOT_NOVEL_IN_FOOD_SUPPLEMENTS"},{"part":"Fruit","status":"NOT_NOVEL_IN_FOOD"}]'::jsonb, 'The fruit (berry) of Carica papaya has been used as food within the EU before 15 May 1997. However, the request concerns the oil of the fruits of Carica papaya . Carica papaya is a small tree (belonging to the plant family Caricaceae), the single stem growing from 5 to 10 m tall, with spirally arranged leaves confined to the top of the trunk the lower trunk is conspicuously scarred where leaves and fruit were borne. The leaves are large, 50-70 cm diameter, deeply palmately lobed with 7 lobes. Th'),
('POL-NF-00219', 'Carissa carandus', null, 'Karonda (LV)', '[{"part":"Leaves, fruits, roots, bark and wood","status":"NOT_NOVEL_IN_FOOD_SUPPLEMENTS"}]'::jsonb, 'The entry concerns the leaves, fruits, roots, bark and wood of Carissa carandus (Karonda).'),
